            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'zh'">
                <p>以下是一个脱敏案例：某企业协作工具团队收到 Google Ads 通知，账户因“异常活动”被暂停。团队最初只提交了一段产品介绍和网站首页截图，数日后仍未恢复。由于看不到具体拒绝代码，团队开始从账户、付款、广告落地页和历史操作记录四个方向重新整理证据。</p>
<p>这次经历说明了一个关键判断：申诉不是解释“产品为什么好”，而是要证明“账户及广告活动符合 Google 的政策和审核要求”。材料越具体，审核人员越容易快速定位事实；一封泛泛的求情邮件，反而可能增加二次沟通成本。</p>
<h2>先确认停用类型，不要把申诉材料用错地方</h2>
<p>收到通知后，先区分三种情况：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>仅广告被拒</strong>：需要修改广告内容、落地页或投放政策违规项，通常重新提交审核即可。</li>
<li><strong>账户进入暂停状态</strong>：应先处理付款异常、异常活动、身份验证或政策警告，避免继续提交无法通过的付款方式。</li>
<li><strong>账户被最终停用</strong>：可通过通知邮件、Google Ads 官方帮助入口或 MCC 中的相关流程提交申诉，但应确认是否仍处于可申诉阶段。</li>
</ul>
<p>案例团队一开始把问题理解为“广告文案被拒”，后来从通知中的账户范围和付款提示才发现，真正触发审核的是整个账户异常。若重新提交的是广告级说明，即使文案修改完成，也无法解决账户级问题。</p>
<h2>一份可提交的材料包，应形成证据链</h2>
<h3>1. 主体与账户身份</h3>
<p>准备企业营业执照、法定代表人和广告账户管理员身份证明，以及 Google Ads 付款资料与公司主体之间的对应关系。中英文主体名称不一致时，补充一份关系说明，例如“海外主体为境内公司的关联公司或收款平台”。</p>
<p>网站归属也能证明经营真实性。可提供域名后台截图、官网关于我们页面、服务条款、退款政策和隐私政策链接。截图应保留域名、页面地址及可见日期，避免只截首页标题。</p>
<h3>2. 付款与充值记录</h3>
<p>若停用原因涉及付款，需要准备真实可核验的交易记录：付款方式末四位、付款币种、交易时间、金额，以及对应的银行账单、信用卡账单或支付平台账单。敏感卡号、完整账号和验证码必须遮盖，但不要遮挡审核所需的匹配字段。</p>
<p>案例团队还提交了支付平台向广告账户的充值记录，发现其中一笔金额与系统显示相差一个币种单位。团队主动说明汇率与手续费来源，避免审核人员把差额误判为不可解释的付款活动。</p>
<h3>3. 产品与落地页证据</h3>
<p>Google 审核的重点不是页面是否华丽，而是用户点击广告后能否获得与广告承诺一致的内容。建议准备：</p>
<ul>
<li>核心产品页面及功能演示；</li>
<li>注册流程、试用说明和价格页面；</li>
<li>客服邮箱、办公地址与工作时间；</li>
<li>隐私政策、服务条款和第三方追踪说明；</li>
<li>下载页、应用商店页面或产品文档；</li>
<li>能够解释业务模式的组织结构或服务流程说明。</li>
</ul>
<p>落地页若存在强制注册、误导性按钮、虚假稀缺提示或与广告不一致的内容，应先修正，再提交申诉。不能仅用一张正常首页截图掩盖被指向的具体违规页面。</p>
<h3>4. 违规记录与整改动作</h3>
<p>不要简单否认所有问题。按照“通知原文—对应证据—已采取措施”的顺序说明，可采用以下结构：</p>
<ol>
<li>确认收到哪一次通知及哪项政策提醒；</li>
<li>说明停用前的异常行为或配置错误；</li>
<li>列出已完成的整改动作；</li>
<li>提供整改后的页面或账户截图；</li>
<li>说明后续如何防止问题再次发生。</li>
</ol>
<p>如果团队无法确认具体违规原因，可以请求进一步说明，但不要猜测政策编号，也不要提交与通知无关的大量营销材料。</p>
<h2>提交申诉后的跟进动作</h2>
<p>申诉正文建议控制在可快速阅读的范围内：第一段说明账户主体和申诉诉求，第二、三段列出事件时间线及整改措施，附件按“身份、付款、网站、产品、政策整改”编号。每个附件使用清晰文件名，并注明对应证据。</p>
<p>提交后保留完整记录，包括提交时间、所用账号、邮件标题和材料版本。不要同时通过多个重复渠道提交内容不同的申诉，也不要因审核等待期短而反复催促。业务侧应同步检查余额、付款方式和广告投放安排，避免账户恢复后因付款失效再次受限。</p>
<p>更稳妥的做法是让 MCC 管理员统一整理账户、付款和权限信息，确认每个管理员都能接收官方通知，并将权限异常、员工离职交接和付款主体变更记录留存备查。</p>
<h2>案例结果：材料通过以后还要做什么</h2>
<p>该团队重新按证据链提交材料后，申诉进入人工审核，最终账户限制被解除。此前被拒的两组广告仍需单独修改并重新送审，账户恢复并不代表所有广告都会自动上线。</p>
<p>这个案例的价值不在于某一次申诉成功，而在于明确了处理顺序：<strong>先识别停用层级，再验证主体与付款，随后修正落地页，最后用时间线说明整改和预防措施。</strong> Google 的最终审核结果无法保证，但材料真实、范围准确且前后一致，通常比只提交品牌介绍更有利于进入人工审核流程。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <p>The following is an anonymized scenario: a collaborative SaaS company received a Google Ads notice stating that its account had been suspended for “suspicious activity.” The team initially submitted only a product description and a screenshot of its homepage. When the account was not restored, the team began reorganizing its evidence across four areas: account identity, payment history, landing pages, and historical account activity.</p>
<p>The key lesson is that an appeal should not merely explain why the product is good. It should demonstrate that the account and its campaigns comply with Google’s policies and verification requirements. Specific evidence helps reviewers understand the facts quickly, while a generic request for reinstatement may create unnecessary back-and-forth.</p>
<h2>First, Identify the Suspension Level</h2>
<p>Before preparing documents, determine which type of enforcement action applies:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ad rejection only:</strong> Review and correct the ad copy, landing page, or policy violation, then resubmit the ad for review.</li>
<li><strong>Account suspension:</strong> Resolve payment issues, suspicious activity, identity verification, or policy warnings before appealing.</li>
<li><strong>Final account termination:</strong> Submit an appeal through the relevant option in the notification email, Google Ads Help resources, or MCC. Confirm that the account is still eligible to appeal.</li>
</ul>
<p>In the SaaS example, the team initially assumed that an ad had been rejected. After reviewing the scope of the notice and the payment-related instructions, it realized that the account itself had been flagged for unusual activity. A response focused only on ad copy would not have addressed the actual enforcement issue.</p>
<h2>Build a Document Package That Tells a Consistent Story</h2>
<h3>1. Business Identity and Account Ownership</h3>
<p>Prepare the company registration certificate, identification for the legal representative and account administrator, and documents showing how the Google Ads payment profile relates to the advertising company. If the English and Chinese business names differ, include a short explanation stating whether they are affiliates, a unified-entity-company arrangement, or another legitimate relationship.</p>
<p>Website ownership can also establish business legitimacy. Useful evidence includes domain-account screenshots, an About page, terms of service, refund policy, privacy policy, and contact details. Screenshots should visibly include the domain, page address, and relevant information rather than showing only a homepage headline.</p>
<h3>2. Payment and Funding Records</h3>
<p>If the suspension involves payment, provide verifiable transaction records, including the last four digits of the payment method, currency, date, amount, and the corresponding bank statement, card statement, or payment-platform invoice. Redact full card numbers, complete bank account numbers, and verification codes, but retain the fields needed to verify a match.</p>
<p>The SaaS team also submitted its top-up history between the payment platform and the ads account. It discovered that one transaction appeared to differ by one currency unit. By documenting the exchange rate and applicable fees, the company prevented the discrepancy from being treated as an unexplained payment event.</p>
<h3>3. Product and Landing-Page Evidence</h3>
<p>Google’s review is not primarily concerned with visual design. Reviewers want to know whether users who click the ad receive content consistent with the ad’s promise. The document package should include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Core product pages and feature demonstrations;</li>
<li>Registration, trial, and pricing information;</li>
<li>Customer-support contacts, business address, and operating hours;</li>
<li>Privacy policy, terms of service, and tracking disclosures;</li>
<li>Download pages, app-store listings, or product documentation;</li>
<li>A description of the operating model and service-delivery process where relevant.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a landing page contains forced registration, misleading buttons, false scarcity claims, or content inconsistent with the ad, fix it before appealing. A screenshot of an otherwise normal homepage should not be used to obscure the specific page cited in the notice.</p>
<h3>4. Violation History and Remediation</h3>
<p>Avoid simply denying every allegation. Organize the response in this sequence: notification, supporting evidence, completed corrective action, and preventive measures.</p>
<p>A practical appeal should:\n<br />
1. Identify the exact notice or policy reminder being addressed;<br />
2. Explain the unusual behavior or configuration error that occurred before suspension;<br />
3. List the corrective actions already completed;<br />
4. Provide screenshots or links showing the updated pages or account configuration;<br />
5. Explain how similar issues will be prevented in the future.</p>
<p>If the team cannot identify the precise violation, it may request clarification. However, it should not guess policy names or submit a large volume of unrelated marketing materials.</p>
<h2>What to Do After Submitting the Appeal</h2>
<p>Keep the appeal concise. The first paragraph can state the business identity and appeal request. The next two can present a timeline and the corrective actions. Label attachments by category: identity, payment, website, product, and policy remediation. Use descriptive filenames and explain what each attachment demonstrates.</p>
<p>Retain the full submission record, including the date, account used, email subject, and document version. Avoid sending different appeals through several channels at the same time, and do not repeatedly chase reviewers during the standard waiting period. The business should also review balances, active payment methods, and campaign settings so that the account is not restricted again after reinstatement due to a failed renewal.</p>
<p>For multi-account management, an MCC administrator should standardize account, billing, and access information. All relevant administrators should be able to receive official notices, while records of permission changes, employee offboarding, and changes in the paying entity should be maintained for future reviews.</p>
<h2>Case Outcome and Next Steps</h2>
<p>After resubmitting the evidence in a consistent sequence, the case entered manual review and the account restriction was lifted. However, two previously rejected ads still had to be edited and resubmitted separately. Restoring the account did not automatically approve every campaign.</p>
<p>The main lesson is not that any particular appeal is guaranteed to succeed. It is the order of operations: identify the enforcement level, verify the business and payment relationship, correct the landing pages, and then explain remediation and prevention through a clear timeline. Google makes the final determination, but accurate, relevant, and internally consistent evidence is more useful than a general description of the product.</p>
